@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: The Constructive Theory of History — Totality, Class Consciousness, Praxis, Subject-Object
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-07-17
PAPERS_FOUND: 18 total (High: 11, Medium: 7, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers the "positive" or constructive half of History and Class
Consciousness (1923): the doctrines Lukács builds to overcome the reification
diagnosed in the essays covered by Domain 2. Four constructive claims anchor
the literature. First, "totality" is presented by Lukács as "the bearer of
the principle of revolution in science" (What Is Orthodox Marxism?): grasping
capitalist society as an internally mediated whole, rather than as a
collection of isolated, reified "facts," is itself the decisive Marxist
method, prior to and independent of any particular economic thesis. Jay's
(1984) landmark intellectual history traces this category's career from
Hegel through Lukács to Adorno and Habermas, showing how "totality" migrates
from an ontological claim about society to a contested methodological
posture; Schmidt (1975) and Alzu (2020) narrow the focus to how the concrete
totality underwrites Lukács's account of proletarian Bildung and to how the
concept of totality itself changes across Lukács's oeuvre (from the "young"
totality-loss narrative, through HCC's totality-as-revolutionary-standpoint,
to the Ontology's altered totality-concept); Thompson (2011) reconstructs
totality specifically as a resource for contemporary critical-theoretical
social ontology.

Second, the "identical subject-object of history": Lukács's boldest and most
contested claim is that the proletariat, in becoming conscious of itself as
a commodity, simultaneously becomes conscious of the totality of capitalist
social relations that produced it, and so can act as both the object
determined by, and the subject transforming, history -- a materialist
inheritance of the role Hegel's Spirit plays in idealism. Westerman (2010)
offers a genealogical reconstruction locating the immediate philosophical
resources for this claim in Lukács's engagement with Husserlian
phenomenology (rather than Hegel alone), while López's (2019) book-length
study reconstructs the concept of praxis as the vehicle that is supposed to
both "bear the weight" of Hegel's Absolute and overcome it -- and argues,
in an immanent critique, that Lukács's praxis remains unconsciously
Hegelian rather than genuinely superseding speculative philosophy.

Third, "imputed" (zugerechnetes) class consciousness: Lukács's technical
distinction between the empirical, psychological consciousness actual
workers have and the class consciousness "imputed" to the proletariat as
the rational response its class position would produce if fully thought
through. Lanning's (2009, 2002) studies are the most sustained
Anglophone treatments of this specific concept, tracing its operation both
in Lukács's political theory and in his literary criticism of realism.

Fourth, the internal "Tailism" debate: Lukács's own unpublished 1925-26
manuscript Tailism and the Dialectic defended HCC against party critics
(principally Rudas and Deborin) while conceding ground on the "dialectics
of nature" question. The 2000 English translation (ed. Žižek, trans.
Leslie) occasioned a cluster of Historical Materialism essays -- Fracchia
(2013) and Burkett (2013) both argue that Lukács's concessions on natural
dialectics and his derivation of proletarian consciousness from party
authority rather than empirical class experience expose deep problems in
his "philosophical Leninism"; Feenberg (2015) responds to both, defending
alternative, less concessive readings of Lukács's position on nature and
consciousness. Moir's (2020) translation and contextualization of Bloch's
1923 review of HCC illuminates the same dialectics-of-nature fault line
from outside the Historical Materialism debate, showing that Bloch --
unlike Lukács -- never abandoned a dialectics of nature, and that both
thinkers nonetheless converged on treating the present as the privileged
locus of revolutionary praxis (the "Archimedean point").

Finally, historical teleology: Menozzi (2022) argues that Lukács
persistently oscillates between a genuinely materialist, open-ended
"anticipatory" teleology of labor (following Bloch's terminology) and a
residual, closed determinism inherited from the Hegelian dialectic he
otherwise criticizes; Jay (2018) examines whether HCC's implicit philosophy
of history -- reading the Russian Revolution as the meaningful climax of a
unified historical narrative -- can survive a century in which no single
group can any longer be identified as history's "subject," contrasting
Lukács's teleological narrative with Badiou's and Žižek's "fidelity to the
Event" alternative. Rockmore's (1992) monograph situates Lukács's
resolution of the antinomies of bourgeois reason within a longer Marxist
argument about the historical, non-transcendent character of reason itself,
providing a philosophical throughline connecting the antinomies (Domain 2)
to the standpoint-of-the-proletariat resolution (this domain). López's
(2017) review essay on the Feenberg/Kavoulakos/Westerman literature
surveys the state of the "philosophy of praxis" interpretive tradition as
a whole, providing a useful orientation point for how the secondary
literature has organized itself around these constructive doctrines.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the positive doctrines the review's overall argument
must reconstruct and assess: whether totality, the identical subject-object,
imputed class consciousness, and the standpoint of the proletariat form a
coherent alternative to the reified, contemplative stance diagnosed in
Domain 2, or merely relocate its antinomies (as Domain 2's Jaffe and López
entries already suggest) onto a philosophy of history that requires a
strong, teleological reading of the proletariat's revolutionary vocation.
The Tailism material additionally supplies primary documentation of
Lukács's own contemporaneous defense and revision of these doctrines against
party critics, essential for assessing how much of the "constructive theory"
survived Lukács's own later self-criticism (addressed further in Domain 1
and Domain 5).

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Anglophone treatments specifically of imputed class consciousness as a
freestanding epistemological/political concept are thin outside Lanning's
two studies; most other treatments of the topic (e.g., Jaffe, Miller, in
Domain 2) approach it only as a corollary of the antinomies or modality
discussion rather than as a topic in its own right. Sustained
book-length treatment of the "Towards a Methodology of the Problem of
Organisation" essay specifically (as opposed to "Class Consciousness" and
"Reification") is largely absent from the literature located here; the
organizational/party dimension of Lukács's constructive theory is
consequently under-represented relative to its epistemological dimension.
Several sources (Lanning's book and 2002 article, López's 2019 book,
Rockmore's 1992 book, the Jay 1984 book) could not be matched to a DOI via
CrossRef and are enriched, where possible, only via PhilPapers/OpenAlex
bibliographic metadata; these are marked INCOMPLETE where no abstract could
be resolved.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize by constructive doctrine rather than chronology: (1) totality as
method (Jay, Schmidt, Alzu, Thompson); (2) identical subject-object and
praxis (Westerman, López 2019, López 2017); (3) imputed class consciousness
(Lanning x2); (4) the Tailism debate on the dialectics of nature and party
authority (primary Tailism text, Fracchia, Burkett, Feenberg, Moir); (5)
teleology and the philosophy of history (Menozzi, Jay 2018, Rockmore).
Cross-reference Domain 2's Jaffe and López entries when assessing whether
the "standpoint of the proletariat" resolves or merely relocates the
antinomies of bourgeois thought, and flag the Tailism cluster for
cross-reference with Domain 1's Congdon (2007) treatment of the same
manuscript from a biographical/periodization angle.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Totality as revolutionary method: Jay (1984), Schmidt (1975), Alzu (2020),
  Thompson (2011) - totality as the methodological core of Lukács's Marxism,
  with disagreement over how far the concept survives into the Ontology.
- Identical subject-object / praxis as overcoming reification: Westerman
  (2010), López (2019) - reconstructive readings that nonetheless diverge on
  whether Lukács's praxis concept escapes or merely reproduces Hegelian
  speculative structure.
- Imputed vs. empirical class consciousness: Lanning (2009, 2002) - the
  concept's operation in political theory and literary criticism.
- The Tailism debate (dialectics of nature, party authority): primary text
  (2000), Fracchia (2013), Burkett (2013), Feenberg (2015), Moir (2020) -
  a live three/four-way debate over how much Lukács conceded to his party
  critics and what this reveals about the philosophical foundations of his
  theory of proletarian consciousness.
- Teleology and the philosophy of history: Menozzi (2022), Jay (2018),
  Rockmore (1992) - whether Lukács's resolution of the antinomies commits
  him to a closed, determinist teleology or an open, anticipatory one.
====================================================================
}

@book{lukacs1971history,
  author = {Lukács, Georg},
  title = {History and Class Consciousness: Studies in Marxist Dialectics},
  year = {1971},
  publisher = {MIT Press},
  address = {Cambridge, MA},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The primary text for this domain. In "What Is Orthodox Marxism?," "Class Consciousness," "Reification and the Consciousness of the Proletariat," and "Towards a Methodology of the Problem of Organisation," Lukács argues that Marxism's distinguishing feature is not any particular economic thesis but "the point of view of totality"; that the proletariat's structural position as both the object of capitalist exchange and the (potential) subject of historical transformation makes it the "identical subject-object of history"; that actual, empirical working-class consciousness must be distinguished from the class consciousness "imputed" to the proletariat by its objective position; and that revolutionary organization and praxis are the practical vehicle through which this imputed consciousness becomes actual, overcoming the contemplative stance of reification diagnosed elsewhere in the volume.

  RELEVANCE: The foundational primary source for every constructive doctrine this domain surveys (totality, subject-object identity, imputed consciousness, praxis, organization); translated by Rodney Livingstone from the 1923 German original, with Lukács's own 1967 self-critical preface (treated at length in Domain 1).

  POSITION: Primary text establishing the "philosophy of praxis" reading of Marxism that virtually all subsequent secondary literature in this domain interprets, defends, or criticizes.
  },
  keywords = {totality, subject-object, class-consciousness, praxis, primary-source, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}

@book{lukacs2000tailism,
  author = {Lukács, Georg},
  editor = {Žižek, Slavoj},
  title = {A Defence of History and Class Consciousness: Tailism and the Dialectic},
  year = {2000},
  publisher = {Verso},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Written in 1925-26 but unpublished until 1996, Lukács's manuscript defends History and Class Consciousness against contemporary party critics (principally Rudas and Deborin), who charged it with idealism, voluntarism, and a rejection of the dialectics of nature. Against the charge of "tailism" (Chvostismus, trailing behind rather than leading revolutionary consciousness), Lukács reasserts the primacy of totality and defends -- while also partially qualifying -- his account of proletarian class consciousness and the party's role in mediating it.

  RELEVANCE: The primary document for this domain's Tailism debate; translated by Esther Leslie with an introductory essay by John Rees and a postface by Slavoj Žižek. Together with the secondary Historical Materialism exchange (Fracchia, Burkett, Feenberg below), it supplies the textual basis for assessing how much of the "constructive theory" Lukács himself was prepared to defend, qualify, or concede under party pressure -- directly bearing on this domain's question of whether the standpoint of the proletariat is philosophically or organizationally (party-)determined.

  POSITION: Primary self-defense; the central textual battleground for the debate (below) over whether Lukács's concessions here undermine or merely refine the constructive theory of HCC.
  },
  keywords = {tailism, class-consciousness, party, dialectics-of-nature, primary-source, High},
  abstract = {"In the mid-1920s, Lukacs wrote Tailism and the Dialectic. Unpublished at the time, Lukacs himself thought the text had been destroyed. However, a group of researchers recently found the manuscript gathering dust in the newly opened archives of the CPSU in Moscow."--Jacket.Includes bibliographical references."In the mid-1920s, Lukacs wrote Tailism and the Dialectic. Unpublished at the time, Lukacs himself thought the text had been destroyed. However, a group of researchers recently found the manuscript gathering dust in the newly opened archives of the CPSU in Moscow."--Jacket},
  abstract_source = {core},
}

@book{jay1984marxismtotality,
  author = {Jay, Martin},
  title = {Marxism and Totality: The Adventures of a Concept from Lukács to Habermas},
  year = {1984},
  publisher = {University of California Press},
  address = {Berkeley},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Jay traces the concept of "totality" through the Western Marxist tradition inaugurated by Lukács -- as a category of Hegelian inheritance repurposed to grasp capitalist society as an internally mediated whole rather than a collection of reified, isolated facts -- and follows its subsequent transformations, contestations, and partial abandonments through Korsch, the Frankfurt School, Sartre, Althusser, and Habermas.

  RELEVANCE: The most sustained intellectual history of exactly the category this domain's first key question addresses -- how totality functions methodologically as "the bearer of the principle of revolution in science" -- and situates Lukács's specific formulation within a much longer and more contested trajectory, showing that later Western Marxists (Althusser above all) came to see the same category as a liability rather than a resource.

  POSITION: Intellectual-historical reconstruction that treats totality as Western Marxism's defining and most contested category, with Lukács's HCC as the decisive originating formulation.
  },
  keywords = {totality, Western-Marxism, intellectual-history, Jay, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}

@article{schmidt1975concretetotality,
  author = {Schmidt, James},
  title = {The Concrete Totality and Lukács' Concept of Proletarian Bildung},
  journal = {Telos},
  year = {1975},
  volume = {1975},
  number = {24},
  pages = {2--40},
  doi = {10.3817/0675024002},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Schmidt examines how Lukács's concept of the "concrete totality" underwrites his account of proletarian Bildung -- the process by which the working class's self-formation as a revolutionary subject is supposed to proceed through practical self-education within, rather than abstraction from, the concrete social totality it inhabits.

  RELEVANCE: Connects this domain's totality category directly to the practical, pedagogical dimension of class-consciousness formation (the "how" of imputed consciousness becoming actual), complementing Lanning's studies of imputed consciousness with an account of totality's role in that same process.

  POSITION: Reads totality not merely as an epistemological category but as the framework for a theory of proletarian self-formation (Bildung) through revolutionary practice.
  },
  keywords = {totality, Bildung, class-formation, Telos, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract A truly critical Marxian analysis of society does not appear full blown in the thunderclap of a coupure epistémologique, leaving all previous efforts at critical reflection on the far side of the “ideology”-“science” opposition. Rather, as a determinate, historical negation of the self-understanding achieved at those moments of bourgeois social theory's most incisive insights into the limits of its society, it reaches its “new continent of thought” at least in part by vehicles from the old world. It should not be surprising then that those Marxists who have clearly perceived the specificity of Marx's achievement have also often been equally sensitive towards this exploration of the limits of bourgeois thought which has taken place in the finest products of classical liberal social theory.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{alzu2020totalidadsocial,
  author = {Alzu, Alejandro Nahuel},
  title = {Consideraciones para una actualización del concepto de totalidad social con base en la obra de Georg Lukács},
  journal = {Estudios de Filosofía},
  year = {2020},
  number = {62},
  doi = {10.17533/udea.ef.n62a05},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Alzu reviews the successive elaborations of the concept of social totality across Lukács's oeuvre -- from the "young" Lukács, through History and Class Consciousness, to Toward the Ontology of Social Being -- and argues that a radical change of position occurs between HCC and the Ontology, driven by the theoretical need to make room for a subjective practice that is not ineluctably alienated (as HCC's model of totality had implied). Alzu concludes that the Ontology's revised concept of totality enables a new, non-dogmatic type of emancipatory practice distinct from the party-centered dogmatism of much twentieth-century socialism.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses this domain's first key question by tracking how "totality" changes function across Lukács's career, and supplies an internal-development account of why the HCC-era totality concept (this domain's focus) required later revision -- a useful bridge to Domain 5's treatment of the Ontology.

  POSITION: Developmental account arguing that Lukács's concept of totality must be revised between HCC and the Ontology to avoid entailing that all subjective practice under totality is necessarily alienated.
  },
  keywords = {totality, ontology, praxis, alienation, Spanish-language, Medium},
  abstract = {En este artículo reviso las diversas elaboraciones del concepto de totalidad social que podemos encontrar en diferentes momentos de la obra del filósofo marxista Georg Lukács y destaco las concepciones de la praxis de los agentes sociales asociadas a cada una de ellas. Comienzo por analizar este concepto en la obra del joven Lukács, para luego presentar un radical cambio de postura entre las formulaciones que hallamos en Historia y conciencia de clase y Para una ontología del ser social. Mi argumento es que este cambio de perspectiva se asocia a la necesidad teórica de posibilitar una práctica subjetiva que no se encuentre indefectiblemente alienada, tal como parece desprenderse del modelo teórico de la obra lukácsiana de juventud. Finalmente concluyo que esta nueva concepción de la totalidad social habilita un nuevo tipo depráctica emancipatoria, alejada de los dogmatismos partidarios que caracterizaron a buena parte del socialismo del siglo XX.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@incollection{thompson2011ontologytotality,
  author = {Thompson, Michael J.},
  editor = {Thompson, Michael J.},
  title = {Ontology and Totality: Reconstructing Lukács' Concept of Critical Theory},
  booktitle = {Georg Lukács Reconsidered: Critical Essays in Politics, Philosophy and Aesthetics},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Continuum},
  address = {London},
  pages = {229--250},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Thompson reconstructs Lukács's category of totality as a resource for contemporary critical theory, arguing that totality's methodological function -- grasping social phenomena as internally related moments of a mediated whole rather than as discrete, positivistically isolable facts -- remains defensible and useful independently of the specific historical-teleological apparatus (the proletariat as revolutionary subject) in which Lukács originally embedded it.

  RELEVANCE: Represents an explicit attempt to detach this domain's central methodological category (totality) from the specific philosophy-of-history commitments (subject-object identity, proletarian teleology) this domain otherwise surveys, posing directly the question of how much of Lukács's constructive apparatus can survive without its original revolutionary-subject commitments.

  POSITION: Reconstructive/appropriative reading that seeks to preserve totality as a critical-theoretical method while bracketing Lukács's specific historical-teleological claims about the proletariat.
  },
  keywords = {totality, critical-theory, ontology, Thompson, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{westerman2010reification,
  author = {Westerman, Richard},
  title = {The Reification of Consciousness: Husserl's Phenomenology in Lukács's Identical Subject-Object},
  journal = {New German Critique},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {37},
  number = {3},
  pages = {97--130},
  doi = {10.1215/0094033X-2010-016},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Westerman argues that the specific philosophical mechanics of Lukács's "identical subject-object" claim -- how the proletariat's coming-to-consciousness of its own commodity status is supposed to simultaneously constitute consciousness of the social totality -- are best reconstructed via Husserlian phenomenology rather than via Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit alone, since it is a phenomenological account of constitution (of objects for a subject) that does the technical work of explaining how self-consciousness and consciousness-of-the-totality could be the same act.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses this domain's second key question -- the logical structure of the identical subject-object claim -- by supplying a genealogically grounded account of its constitutive mechanics, complicating readings (including López's, below) that treat the concept as derived primarily from Hegel.

  POSITION: Phenomenological-genealogical reconstruction of the identical subject-object thesis, tracing its technical apparatus to Husserl (via Lukács's engagement with contemporary phenomenology) rather than to Hegelian dialectics alone.
  },
  keywords = {identical-subject-object, Husserl, phenomenology, Westerman, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}

@book{lopez2019praxisabsolute,
  author = {López, Daniel Andrés},
  title = {Lukács: Praxis and the Absolute},
  year = {2019},
  publisher = {Brill},
  address = {Leiden},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: López reconstructs Lukács's philosophy of praxis as a conceptual-historical totality built on an explicitly Hegelian basis, arguing that Lukács's concept of praxis (as the culmination of mediation, logic, and genesis) is designed to both bear the full weight of Hegel's Absolute and to overcome it. López's immanent critique concludes that Lukács does not succeed in overcoming speculative Hegelian philosophy: his praxis concept remains -- unconsciously -- within the structure it claims to supersede, since Lukács's insight into praxis is won only retrospectively, after the historical event on which it is built, in the manner of Hegelian philosophical reflection rather than as a living, present truth.

  RELEVANCE: A book-length treatment of exactly this domain's second key question (the logical structure and philosophical cost of the subject-object/praxis claim) that reaches a critical conclusion: the "identical subject-object" and the praxis concept built to sustain it do not escape Hegelian speculative structure, raising the same immanent-critique concerns that Domain 2's López (2020) and Jaffe (2020) entries raise about the antinomies and the standpoint of the proletariat.

  POSITION: Sympathetic but ultimately critical immanent reconstruction: defends Lukács's philosophy of praxis as a serious and internally coherent project while arguing it fails, on its own terms, to overcome the Hegelian Absolute it was built to supersede.
  },
  keywords = {praxis, Hegel, Absolute, subject-object, López, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{lopez2017returnpraxis,
  author = {López, Daniel},
  title = {A Return to the Philosophy of Praxis},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {25},
  number = {4},
  pages = {257--282},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206X-12341550},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: A review essay on Feenberg's The Philosophy of Praxis, offering a critical yet sympathetic summary of Feenberg's argument (developed via Marx, Lukács, and Marcuse) before arguing that Feenberg's treatment of Lukács's 1920s work is unparalleled in countering the systematic distortion to which it has been subject, particularly Feenberg's defense of Lukács's ontology of nature and his politics against the charge of authoritarianism. López suggests, however, that Feenberg elides the analysis of the state and politics in Marx and Lukács, and proposes instead a Hegelian interpretation of Lukács's concept of mediation parallel to the Phenomenology of Spirit.

  RELEVANCE: Provides an orientation point for how the secondary literature surveyed throughout this domain (Feenberg above all) has organized itself around the "philosophy of praxis" label and around the authoritarianism charge central to the Tailism debate (Fracchia, Burkett, Feenberg below), useful for the synthesis phase in mapping the state of the field.

  POSITION: Critical but sympathetic review of Feenberg's philosophy-of-praxis reconstruction, written from within the same interpretive tradition López's own 2019 monograph develops and criticizes via a rival Hegelian reading of mediation.
  },
  keywords = {praxis, philosophy-of-praxis, review-essay, López, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract I review The Philosophy of Praxis by Andrew Feenberg, firstly, presenting a critical yet sympathetic summary of Feenberg’s argument, developed via Marx, Lukács and Marcuse. Despite sharing Adorno’s and Marcuse’s dismissal of proletarian revolution, he finds aspects of Marx and particularly Lukács compelling. Upon this synthesis he builds his own philosophy. Secondly, I argue that Feenberg’s treatment of Lukács’s 1920s work is unparalleled and may counter the systematic distortion to which it has been subject. He defends Lukács’s ontology with respect to nature and his politics, countering the charge of authoritarianism. Finally, I suggest that Feenberg tends to elide the analysis of the state and politics in Marx and Lukács, and that this distorts his understanding of the latter. I suggest a Hegelian interpretation of Lukács’s concept of mediation by way of parallel with The Phenomenology of Spirit . I conclude by suggesting the implications of this disagreement.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{lanning2009organizingclassconsciousness,
  author = {Lanning, Robert},
  title = {Georg Lukács and Organizing Class Consciousness},
  year = {2009},
  publisher = {MEP Publications},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lanning offers a sustained study of Lukács's concept of imputed (zugerechnetes) class consciousness, tracing its operation across "historical necessity as self-activity," the distinction between class experience and false consciousness, common-sense/market rationality in sociological studies of class, and the development of imputed consciousness within the individual, arguing that this technical distinction -- rather than reification alone -- is the organizing category of Lukács's political theory of class formation.

  RELEVANCE: The most sustained book-length Anglophone treatment located of exactly this domain's third key question: what imputed class consciousness is and how it relates to empirical, psychological proletarian consciousness. Provides the fullest reconstruction of how the concept is meant to organize political practice rather than remain a purely epistemological postulate.

  POSITION: Systematic reconstruction and defense of imputed class consciousness as Lukács's central organizing category, read against sociological accounts that treat class consciousness as a purely empirical, survey-measurable phenomenon.
  },
  keywords = {imputed-class-consciousness, class-formation, Lanning, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
}

@article{lanning2002imputedconsciousness,
  author = {Lanning, Robert},
  title = {Lukács's Concept of Imputed Consciousness in Realist Literature},
  journal = {Nature, Society, and Thought},
  year = {2002},
  volume = {15},
  number = {2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lanning traces how the concept of imputed class consciousness -- developed philosophically in History and Class Consciousness -- operates in Lukács's later literary criticism, arguing that Lukács's preference for realist over modernist literature is grounded in the claim that realist form alone can represent the gap between characters' empirical consciousness and the imputed consciousness appropriate to their objective class position.

  RELEVANCE: Extends this domain's treatment of imputed class consciousness from political theory into Lukács's aesthetics, showing the concept's continuity across the disciplinary boundary between HCC-era political philosophy and the later literary-critical writings, and thereby supplying a link between this domain and the aesthetic dimension of Lukács's thought that falls outside this review's other domains.

  POSITION: Continuity thesis connecting the HCC-era concept of imputed consciousness to Lukács's later realist aesthetics, read as two applications of a single underlying theoretical distinction.
  },
  keywords = {imputed-class-consciousness, realism, aesthetics, Lanning, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{fracchia2013philosophicalleninism,
  author = {Fracchia, Joseph},
  title = {The Philosophical Leninism and Eastern 'Western Marxism' of Georg Lukács},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2013},
  volume = {21},
  number = {1},
  pages = {69--93},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206X-12341282},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reviewing the English translation of Tailism and the Dialectic, Fracchia argues that Lukács -- generally heralded as a founder of a "Western Marxism" opposed to "Eastern" Soviet orthodoxy -- in fact remains, on the evidence of this text, a "philosophical Leninist": Lukács's purely conceptual derivation of reification as the necessary, immediate form of working-class consciousness leads him to conclude that workers can never attain proper class consciousness on their own, so that this consciousness must instead be attributed to the revolutionary party bearing the correct theory. Fracchia criticizes both the methodological shortcuts in this conceptual derivation and the politically dangerous consequence that the party's theoretical authority is thereby guaranteed against working-class self-activity.

  RELEVANCE: Directly targets this domain's fourth key question by locating, in Lukács's own defense of HCC, textual evidence that "imputed" class consciousness collapses into party-imputed consciousness -- a considerably more authoritarian reading of the concept than Lanning's (above), and central to the domain's internal Tailism debate together with Burkett and Feenberg below.

  POSITION: Critical reading arguing that Tailism and the Dialectic exposes an authoritarian, party-centered "philosophical Leninism" beneath Lukács's reputation as a founder of anti-Leninist Western Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {tailism, imputed-class-consciousness, party, Leninism, Fracchia, High},
  abstract = {Abstract This essay centres on the English translation (2000) of Georg Lukács’s Tailism and the Dialectic (written in either 1925 or 1926). Lukács is generally heralded as a founding theoretician of a ‘Western Marxism’, in opposition to ‘Eastern’ Soviet Marxism, and his most impressive and most influential work, History and Class Consciousness (1923), is generally treated as having rehabilitated Marxist concern with questions of subjectivity. It might therefore come as a surprise when Lukács in Tailism states that the purpose of History and Class Consciousness was to demonstrate ‘that the organisation and tactics of Bolshevism are the only possible consequence of Marxism’. In my view, however, this should already be abundantly clear from History and Class Consciousness . For Lukács’s absorption with proletarian subjectivity was motivated by an obsession with what he saw as its immaturity. And he coined the category of ‘reification’ in order to explain his disappointed expectations, to explain, that is, why the proletariat did not make a ‘socialist’ revolution in the ‘objectively ripe’ situation of an ‘imperialist war’ created by ‘moribund capitalism’. In short, Lukács did raise anew the question of the subjective, but only to then declare that workers, not even ‘the most revolutionary among them’, could never attain proper class consciousness, which he attributed instead to the ‘revolutionary party’ bearing the properly revolutionary theory. For this reason I agree with Slavoj Žižek’s characterisation of Lukács as the ‘ultimate philosopher [my emphasis] of Leninism’ – although I do think that Lenin himself would have found, as he did in connection with one of Lukács’s other works, Marxism ‘present only at a verbal level’. My concern is two-fold: with a critique of the methodological short-cuts that Lukács made in his purely conceptual derivation of the concept of reification, and his purely conceptual attribution of it as the necessary form of working-class consciousness ‘in its immediacy’; and with the dangerous political consequences that Lukács derived from his assessment of the reified character of working-class subjectivity, mainly a theoretical guarantee that the party with the proper revolutionary theory must always be right, or at least more right than anyone else.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{burkett2013lukacsscience,
  author = {Burkett, Paul},
  title = {Lukács on Science: A New Act in the Tragedy},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2013},
  volume = {21},
  number = {3},
  pages = {3--15},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206X-12341313},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Burkett shows that in Tailism and the Dialectic, Lukács -- despite Western Marxism's general rejection of Engels's "dialectics of nature" as one of its defining features -- in fact strongly endorses the existence of an objective dialectic operating in nature, a position in tension with HCC's original restriction of dialectical method to the social realm. Burkett argues that a close reading of Lukács's writings on science nonetheless shows that he continued, in practice, to deny that dialectical method could be meaningfully applied to natural-scientific inquiry, leaving an unresolved contradiction in his position.

  RELEVANCE: Supplies primary textual evidence, internal to the Tailism debate this domain foregrounds, for a specific tension in Lukács's defense of HCC -- between his professed (concessive) endorsement of a dialectics of nature and his continued practical restriction of dialectic to history and society -- directly relevant to assessing how far Lukács's "constructive theory" extends beyond the social-historical domain.

  POSITION: Identifies an unresolved internal contradiction in Lukács's Tailism-era position on the scope of dialectical method (nature versus society), complicating any simple contrast between Lukács's "Western" and orthodox Soviet Marxism on this question.
  },
  keywords = {tailism, dialectics-of-nature, Burkett, High},
  abstract = {Abstract The rejection of the ‘dialectics of nature’ has long been thought of as the most fundamental factor distinguishing Western Marxism from official Soviet-style Marxism. Yet, in Tailism and the Dialectic , Georg Lukács – perhaps the most influential figure in Western Marxism – strongly endorses the existence of an objective dialectic in nature. A close examination of Lukács’s main writings on science shows, however, that he still in effect denied the possibility of applying dialectical method to nature. This paradox is bound up with a dualistic conception of natural and social science with distinctly adverse implications for the development of an ecological Marxism.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{feenberg2015fracchiaburkett,
  author = {Feenberg, Andrew},
  title = {Fracchia and Burkett on Tailism and the Dialectic: A Response},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {23},
  number = {2},
  pages = {228--238},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206X-12341405},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Feenberg responds to Fracchia's and Burkett's critiques (above) of Tailism and the Dialectic, addressing in particular Lukács's theory of class consciousness and his views on the social construction of nature, and develops alternative interpretations of Lukács's positions that resist both the "philosophical Leninism" charge and the charge of an unresolved contradiction over the dialectics of nature.

  RELEVANCE: Completes the three-way Historical Materialism exchange on Tailism and the Dialectic that forms the core of this domain's treatment of the internal Tailism debate, providing the defensive counter-reading against which Fracchia's and Burkett's critiques must be assessed.

  POSITION: Defends Lukács's Tailism-era positions against the party-authoritarianism and nature-dialectics critiques, offering a more sympathetic (though not uncritical) reading than Fracchia or Burkett.
  },
  keywords = {tailism, dialectics-of-nature, class-consciousness, Feenberg, High},
  abstract = {This commentary addresses criticism of Lukács’s early book Tailism and the Dialectic: A Defence of History and Class Consciousness. Two critiques published in Historical Materialism are analysed and alternative interpretations of Lukács’s theory developed. The commentary focuses on Lukács’s theories of class consciousness and his ideas on the social construction of nature.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{moir2020archimedeanpoint,
  author = {Moir, Cat},
  title = {The Archimedean Point: Consciousness, Praxis, and the Present in Lukács and Bloch},
  journal = {Thesis Eleven},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {157},
  number = {1},
  pages = {3--23},
  doi = {10.1177/0725513620911790},
  abstract = {This article consists of an original translation of Ernst Bloch's 1923 review of Lukács History and Class Consciousness, preceded by a translator's introduction contextualising Bloch's review and interpreting what it tells us about the intellectual and personal relationship between Bloch and Lukács. I argue that Bloch's review highlights some of the key differences and points of intersection between their thinking. Written when their personal relationship had already soured for both political and intellectual reasons, Bloch's review makes clear his ongoing commitment to a form of utopianism Lukács had long abandoned. Unlike Lukács, who in History and Class Consciousness argued that the dialectical method could only be applied to the social realm, Bloch followed Engels in developing a dialectics of nature. However, even if Bloch was less willing to become involved in party politics than his erstwhile friend, as the review reveals, both men ultimately emphasised the present moment as the privileged locus of urgent political action.},
  abstract_source = {semanticscholar},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Moir's introduction and translation of Ernst Bloch's contemporaneous 1923 review of History and Class Consciousness shows that Bloch, unlike Lukács, never restricted dialectical method to the social realm and retained a Blochian, utopian dialectics of nature that Lukács's HCC-era position explicitly rejected (a rejection later partially reversed in Tailism, per Burkett above). Despite this disagreement and their by-then-soured friendship, Moir argues both thinkers converged on treating the present moment -- the "Archimedean point" -- as the privileged site of revolutionary praxis.

  RELEVANCE: Provides primary-source (in translation) contemporaneous reception evidence bearing directly on the dialectics-of-nature question central to the Tailism debate, and on this domain's concern with the temporal/teleological structure of Lukácsian praxis (the "present" as the point from which totality becomes graspable and actionable).

  POSITION: Reception-historical/translation study situating Lukács's praxis concept against Bloch's rival, nature-inclusive dialectical utopianism, while identifying a shared emphasis on present-oriented revolutionary action.
  },
  keywords = {tailism, dialectics-of-nature, Bloch, praxis, Moir, Medium}
}

@article{menozzi2022readinghegel,
  author = {Menozzi, Filippo},
  title = {Reading Hegel after Marx: Lukács and the Question of Teleology},
  journal = {International Critical Thought},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {12},
  number = {1},
  pages = {98--115},
  doi = {10.1080/21598282.2022.2035792},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT This paper offers a rethinking of the concept of teleology in Marxist theory. In particular, I propose some reflections on György Lukács's teleology of labour, addressed in The Young Hegel and subsequently reworked in The Ontology of Social Being. Lukács challenged an idealist notion of teleology understood as realisation of a transcendental principle posited a priori. He redefined the concept by showing how Hegel and Marx reintroduced the question of purpose as an essential quality of human labour. Against idealist conceptions, Lukács reimagined teleology as a secular purpose inherent to human praxis and the key to thinking agency within a materialist concept of history. Accordingly, a Marxist concept of teleology should highlight what Ernst Bloch described as the "anticipatory" character of consciousness, whereby teleology means the positing of an end that does not yet exist in reality and that exceeds the temporal horizon of the present. However, in his critique of Hegel, Lukács illustrates the ambivalent and contradictory dimension of teleology, a perspective that constantly relapses into temporal closure and determinism. While proposing a radical reading of Hegel, Lukács oscillates between the two extremes of a dialectical notion of teleology that he nonetheless helped to formulate.},
  abstract_source = {semanticscholar},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Menozzi argues that Lukács's concept of teleology -- developed in The Young Hegel and reworked in the later Ontology of Social Being -- oscillates between two incompatible poles: a genuinely materialist, "anticipatory" teleology of labor (positing an end not yet real, following Bloch's terminology) that would make agency and open-ended historical possibility central to Marxist theory, and a residual, closed determinism inherited from the very Hegelian dialectic Lukács otherwise criticizes for idealist teleology.

  RELEVANCE: Directly answers this domain's fourth key question -- whether Lukács's account entails teleology, and of what kind -- with a nuanced "both/and" answer: Lukács's teleology is neither simply Hegelian-determinist nor simply open-ended, but an unstable oscillation between the two that Lukács himself never resolves.

  POSITION: Diagnoses an internal, unresolved tension in Lukács's concept of historical teleology between anticipatory/open and determinist/closed poles, using Bloch's account of anticipatory consciousness as the normative standard against which Lukács's ambivalence is measured.
  },
  keywords = {teleology, labor, Hegel, Bloch, Menozzi, High}
}

@article{jay2018fidelitytoevent,
  author = {Jay, Martin},
  title = {Fidelity to the Event? Lukács' History and Class Consciousness and the Russian Revolution},
  journal = {Studies in East European Thought},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {70},
  number = {2-3},
  pages = {195--213},
  doi = {10.1007/s11212-018-9307-3},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Jay argues that History and Class Consciousness presupposes that "history" can be understood as a unified, meaningful meta-narrative -- readable, structurally, like a realist novel -- in which the present contains "objective possibilities" that the proletariat, as history's destined agent, can identify and realize through revolutionary intervention. Jay contends that in the century since the Russian Revolution it has become impossible to read history as a singular, triumphalist narrative culminating in emancipation, or to identify any single group as its subjective agent, and examines attempts (by Badiou and Žižek) to salvage Lukács's revolutionary potential through a non-teleological "fidelity to the Event" that replaces historical necessity with contingent, retroactively constituted commitment.

  RELEVANCE: Addresses this domain's fourth key question at the level of narrative form: Lukács's constructive theory requires treating history as having a single meaningful direction and a single collective subject, and Jay assesses whether later attempts to detach the revolutionary charge of HCC from this teleological narrative structure (Badiou's, Žižek's "Event") actually succeed or merely substitute one figural device (Erich Auerbach's figura) for another.

  POSITION: Skeptical historical-critical reading arguing that Lukács's theory of history is inseparable from a teleological, singular-subject narrative structure that cannot survive translation into non-teleological, "Event"-based frameworks without significant loss.
  },
  keywords = {teleology, historical-narrative, Badiou, Žižek, Jay, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{rockmore1992irrationalism,
  author = {Rockmore, Tom},
  title = {Irrationalism: Lukács and the Marxist View of Reason},
  year = {1992},
  publisher = {Temple University Press},
  address = {Philadelphia},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Rockmore situates Lukács's account of reason -- both his critique of bourgeois "irrationalism" (elaborated later in The Destruction of Reason) and his own constructive resolution of the antinomies of bourgeois thought via the standpoint of the proletariat -- within a broader Marxist thesis that reason is historically and socially conditioned (a form of contextualism) rather than a fixed, transcendent faculty, and assesses how far this historicized conception of reason can underwrite Lukács's confidence in the proletariat's capacity to grasp the social totality rationally.

  RELEVANCE: Connects this domain's resolution of the antinomies (the standpoint of the proletariat, treated at length in Domain 2's Jaffe and López entries) to a broader argument about the historically conditioned status of reason itself, supplying philosophical grounding for why Lukács thinks a specific social position (the proletariat's) could yield superior rational insight into the totality.

  POSITION: Reconstructs Lukács's theory of reason as a historicized, contextualist alternative to both bourgeois transcendent rationalism and party-orthodox scientism, evaluating its coherence as a properly Marxist epistemology.
  },
  keywords = {reason, antinomies, standpoint, Rockmore, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}
